{{also|Astrakhan}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Named from the Central-Asian city and region [[w:Astrakhan|Astrakhan]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/æstrəˈkæn/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Closely-curled black or [[grey]] [[fleece]] of very young [[karakul]] [[lamb]]s from [[Astrakhan]].
# [[cloth|Cloth]] resembling the above mentioned [[fur]], often made from [[wool]] and [[mohair]] and used for [[trimmings]].

====Quotations====
#: ''He was dressed in uniform--a black uniform trimmed with gray '''astrakhan.''''' - Richard Connell's "The Most Dangerous Game"
* '''1891''' : [[w:Sir Arthur Conan Doyle|Sir Arthur Conan Doyle]], ''[[w:A Scandal In Bohemia|The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes]]''
*: Heavy bands of '''astrakhan''' were slashed across the sleeves and fronts of his double-breasted coat....
* '''1914''' : [[w:James Joyce|James Joyce]], ''[[w:The Dead (short story)|The Dead]]''
*: He was dressed in a long green overcoat with mock '''astrakhan''' cuffs and collar and wore on his head an oval fur cap.
* '''1929,''' [[w:Virginia Woolf|Virginia Woolf]], ''[[w:A Room of One's Own|A Room of One's Own]]'', Penguin Books, paperback edition, page 33
*: Was the cavalry officer slim and elegant, and dressed in '''astrakhan'''?

